{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, i. 3.}}
<poem>He fought his doubts and gather’d strength,
He would not make his judgment ''blind''.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, xcvi.}}}}
'''3.''' Not directed or governed by sight, physical or mental; not proceeding from or controlled by reason: as, ''blind'' groping; ''blind'' tenacity.
{{smaller block|That which is thought to have done the Bishops hurt, is their going about to bring men to a ''blind'' obedience.
{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 23.}}}}
Specifically—'''4.''' Undiscriminating; heedless; inconsiderate; unreflecting; headlong.
{{smaller block|His feare of God may be as faulty as a ''blind'' zeale.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, ix.}}
This plan is recommended neither to ''blind'' approbation nor to ''blind'' reprobation. {{right|''Jay''.}}}}
